                    },
                    "message": "Adding phosphor-fan-presence and bmcweb maintainers for CC/review."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "friendly ping for review, do we want to consider this patch or should i abandon it instead.."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/README.md",
                    "line": 24,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Not sure I understand the rationale for adding this type specificly.  We already have line 21 and line 25 that cover this."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/README.md",
                    "line": 24,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Documenting fine grained associations was requested by bmcweb maintainers\n\nhttps://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/bmcweb/+/87061/comment/e84ddcd4_2aa8b2b7/\n\nwhich was the reason for opening this patch in the first place.\n\n> We already have line 21 and line 25 that cover this.\n\nThe mapper does not understand `Item`. The mapper (and redfish/ipmi implementations) work in terms of concrete things like `Item.Chassis`, `Item.Fan`, `Item.Drive`, ...\n\nNone of these can understand generic `Item`.\n\nIt may be useful conceptually or as documentation to talk about `Item` but it is not sufficient to actually implement anything."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/README.md",
                    "line": 24,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> The mapper does not understand Item. The mapper (and redfish/ipmi implementations) work in terms of concrete things like Item.Chassis, Item.Fan, Item.Drive, ...\n\n> None of these can understand generic Item.\n\nI have no idea what you are talking about.  Please look at:\n\nyaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item.interface.yaml\n\nThis is a \"real\" \"concrete\" interface.  If mapper can't find those, we have a serious problem."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/README.md",
                    "line": 24,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Maybe i did not communicate precisely, sorry about that.\n\nI am not talking about any technical problem with the mapper, the problem at hand is that the documentation in the PDI is not precise when it comes to which associations should be used to create and query the fan inventory."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/README.md",
                    "line": 24,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "You're right.  PDI should not be precise about an implementation decision like that.  Like I said, we already have it covered by line 21 and 25 because a Fan and a Chassis is also an Item.  You added the clarifying comment to Fan; I don't know why we need it in README also.\n\nAny Item can be cooled_by a fan; you just have a special relationship between Chassis and Fans, which you've already documented.  Adding it to the README doesn't yield extra information but it adds extra characters."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/README.md",
                    "line": 24,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> You're right.  PDI should not be precise about an implementation decision like that.  \n\nThat's something you should probably discuss with Gunnar in the relevant bmcweb change where precise documentation in PDI was requested.\n\n> PDI should not be precise about an implementation decision like that. \n\nSee lines 25 through 35 where the intended associations are documented for *specific* inventory items. Also line 15 through 17 mention that documenting these associations is the design intent.\n\n\n> DBus objects with `xyz.opebmc_project.Inventory.Item.${ItemType}` interface\nmight be associated with other inventory objects. Such association can be\ntranslated to Redfish schemas and then indicate the system topology.\n\n\n> You added the clarifying comment to Fan; I don't know why we need it in README also.\n\nMentioning specific items here in `README.md` is the existing pattern, see lines 25 through 34. While the comments have some overlap, the diff for `README.md` covers the associations between inventory items while the diff in `Fan.interface.yaml` goes more into detail as to which Redfish properties can be filled.\n\nIf we want to change the existing pattern here, that would probably be a separate and unrelated change."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/README.md",
                    "line": 24,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> Mentioning specific items here in README.md is the existing pattern, see lines 25 through 34. \n\nThat is the \"existing pattern\" because those are ones that are actually documented in _those_ interface files.\n\nThere is no 'containing' nor 'cooled_by' relationship documented in Chassis.\n\n> If we want to change the existing pattern here, that would probably be a separate and unrelated change.\n\nYou're right.  I'm deleting this entire section.  There is zero reason for us to be dual-documenting associations and this list is pointless at this point.  I'll do that and then you can rebase this commit."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/README.md",
                    "line": 24,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "remote:   https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-dbus-interfaces/+/90859 Item: README: remove redundant documentation [NEW]"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/README.md",
                    "line": 24,
                    "reviewer": {
